-# Buffered write-error characterization
+# Buffered write-error regression
 
 Run `python3 tests/block-write-errors/run.py` from the ELKS tree. Add
 `--base-core` to use queue, completion and buffer functions from the pinned
@@ -17,11 +17,17 @@ The asynchronous build checks that code path but does not simulate interrupts
 or concurrent writes. The fixture uses two-sector (1 KiB) buffers, not every FAT
 sector-size/cache configuration. Mounted-filesystem unmount is not executed.
 
-These are characterization assertions, not tests claiming a fix. Both current
-and pinned-base core behavior clear dirty state at queue insertion, mark a
-failed buffer not up to date, log an I/O error, return zero from `sys_sync`, and
-provide no error result from final release. Subsequent sync skips that clean
-invalid buffer. An ambiguous completion may already have changed sector bytes.
+The candidate retains a per-device error bit when a WRITE request fails. The
+bit survives buffer reuse and successful later I/O. `fsync_dev` and the kernel
+`sys_sync` return `-EIO` for that device (or any affected device for global sync).
+The pinned-base mode instead characterizes the old zero return. Both versions
+clear dirty state at queue insertion and invalidate failed buffers; neither
+retries an ambiguous write. Only reboot clears the candidate's error bits.
+
+This fixture does not exercise VFS unmount, PicoMEM protocol RELEASE or the
+userspace `sync` program. The libc `sync()` interface remains void, so its
+command-line exit status does not expose the new kernel result. Separate VFS
+and protocol tests are required for unmount and release error handling.
 
 Do not infer that a logged write error permits safe media removal, or that
 re-dirtying buffers alone supplies error propagation or safe retry policy.
